= Determination of the pulse shape for CO molecule

Two low-lying excitations observed in broad spectra obtained from `1/`

 - excitation reaction to $y$ pulse direction,
   equivalent with $z$ direction, at energy $epsilon _ y = 5.936$
 - excitation reaction to $x$ pulse direction,
   at energy $epsilon _ x = 10.114$

#image("1/spectrum.svg")

In order to excite the oscillation selectively, apply pulse centered around the energy - 
need to determine the wavelength in nm.

$
	E = h f = h c / lambda \
	lambda = (h c)/E = (4.135 "eV fs" times 2.997 times 10^(2) "nm/fs")/E approx
	1239 "eV nm" / E
$

So $lambda_x approx 122.529 "nm"$, $lambda_y approx 208.770 "nm"$.

In order to get a reasonably selective excitation, aim for pulse spread of cca 4 eV. From the
Gaussian inversion in FT
$
	sigma_f = sigma_E/h = 1/sigma_t \
	sigma_t = h/sigma_E approx 1.00 "fs"
$
Where I used $sigma_E = 4.135 "eV"$ to get a numerical cancelation.

It is reasonable to give the pulse time of $3 sigma_t$ before running into full force and we shall
count the target time from this full force time, meaning that the propagation time should be 23 fs
with pulse $T_0 = 3.00 "fs"$. We should try varying the intensity until the moment amplitude
in atomic units approaches reasonable fractions (percents) of unity.

